### card homework-8 "Problem Set 8"

	*Due Thursday, November 12th at 11:59 PM*
	
	Complete the following problems and submit them as a PDF to Gradescope. You should show enough work that there is no question about the mathematical process used to obtain your answers, and so that your peers in the class could easily follow along. I encourage you to collaborate with your classmates, so long as you write up your solutions independently. Using AI tools to assist with the homework is not permitted.
	
	At this point, please feel free to do row reduction with technology, like <a href="https://www.wolframalpha.com/">Wolfram Alpha</a> --- note that you should still be doing the setup and all the rest of the problem on your own.

	In problems 3#, determine if the vectors are linearly dependent or independent. If they are dependent, find a nontrivial linear combination (meaning at least one coefficient is nonzero) equal to $\vec{0}$.

	#. $\vec{v_1} = [[ 1 ; 2 ; 3 ]], \ \vec{v_2} = [[ 1 ; 0 ; 2 ]], \ \vec{v_3} = [[ 1 ; 4 ; 4 ]]$.

	**Solution:** Row reducing the matrix with these vectors as columns results in

	$$
		[[ 1, 0, 2 ; 0, 1, -1 ; 0, 0, 0 ]],
	$$

	and the lack of a third pivot column means these are linearly dependent. With $c_3 = t$, we have $c_1 = -2t$ and $c_2 = t$, so if $t = 1$,

	$$
		-2[[ 1 ; 2 ; 3 ]] + [[ 1 ; 0 ; 2 ]] + [[ 1 ; 4 ; 4 ]] = [[ 0 ; 0 ; 0 ]].
	$$

	#. $\vec{v_1} = [[ 1 ; 1 ; 2 ]], \ \vec{v_2} = [[ 2 ; 0 ; 3 ]], \ \vec{v_3} = [[ 4 ; - 1 ; 2 ]]$.

	**Solution:** This time, the matrix reduces to the identity, so the only solution is $c_1 = c_2 = c_3 = 0$, and the vectors are linearly independent.

	#. $\vec{v_1} = [[ 1 ; -1 ; 1 ]], \ \vec{v_2} = [[ 2 ; -9 ; 1 ]], \ \vec{v_3} = [[ 1 ; 1 ; 1 ]], \ \vec{v_4} = [[ 2 ; 0 ; 3 ]]$.

	**Solution:** Since these are four vectors in $#R#^3$, they must be linearly dependent, but we still need to row reduce to find the linear combination. The matrix reduces to

	$$
		[[ 1, 0, 0, \frac{13}{2} ; 0, 1, 0, -1 ; 0, 0, 1, -\frac{5}{2} ]]
	$$

	The fourth column has no leading $1$, so we let $c_4 = t$, and with $t = 2$,

	$$
		-13[[ 1 ; -1 ; 1 ]] + 2[[ 2 ; -9 ; 1 ]] + 5[[ 1 ; 1 ; 1 ]] + 2[[ 2 ; 0 ; 3 ]] = [[ 0 ; 0 ; 0 ]].
	$$

	#. $\vec{v_1} = [[ 1 ; 5 ; 6 ; -10 ; \frac{17}{2} ]], \ \vec{v_2} = [[ 3 ; 1 ; 7 ; -100 ; 0]], \ \vec{v_3} = [[ 0 ; 0 ; 0 ; 0 ; 0 ]], \ \vec{v_4} = [[ 4 ; 1 ; 2 ; 0 ; 89 ]]$. (Please do this without technology!)

	**Solution:** No row reduction necessary here! Since $\vec{v_3} = \vec{0}$, we can put a nonzero coefficient on it and zero on everything else:

	$$
		0\vec{v_1} + 0\vec{v_2} + 1\vec{v_3} + 0\vec{v_4} = \vec{0}.
	$$



